# Feature maturity = definition of done

For every language, toolchain, IDE, or product feature, the **default goal is a path to full maturity / production-ready**. Do not call a feature “implemented”, “done”, or “complete” until that bar is met (or the user explicitly accepts a narrower scoped increment).

## Production-ready means all of these

1. **Core behavior** — Happy path, edge cases, and rejection/error cases match the requirement / BDD scenario.
2. **Tests** — Unit (and golden/acceptance where the repo uses them); CI green; no test that inherits unrelated monorepo `pys.lock` / deps (see CER-001 §4).
3. **Tooling surface** — Lexer/parser/sem/emit (as applicable), plus IDE: highlighting, go-to on the symbols users navigate, hover/completions/snippets where the feature introduces keywords or types — at least parity with similar existing features (e.g. class).
4. **Diagnostics maturity** — When a feature emits diagnostics: **Error** and/or **Warning** as appropriate, actionable **tips**, and **quick fixes** when a `suggested_fix` / edit is available (analyzer JSON + IDE CodeAction). Do not ship a warning-only rule without IDE Warning severity + tip/quick-fix plumbing.
5. **Docs & teaching** — LANGUAGE / EBNF / tutorials updated when the user-facing surface changes.
6. **Beginner book** — When language specs change (grammar, semantics, keywords, diagnostics students see, casing/ordering rules, or runnable patterns), update the beginner book under `book/` so chapters and ` ```pys ` examples stay **syntactically and pedagogically accurate**. Rebuild static HTML (`python book/build_html.py` → `book/html/`) in the **same change set**. Do not claim DoD if the book still teaches pre-behavior or invented syntax.
7. **Teaching completeness (book / tutorials / lessons)** — An instruction, lesson, or tutorial chapter is **not done** until every concept it names is **explained** and illustrated with **good examples** that cover more than the golden/simple path (at least one contrasting or edge situation where it matters). Progressive disclosure: **do not use a concept in examples or exercises before it has been taught** (earlier in the same chapter, or in a prior chapter in `book/SUMMARY.md` order). When you must mention a general programming idea (e.g. string interpolation, modulo, remainder) that is not the chapter’s focus: add a short **sidebar** (about ≤5 lines) in place, **or** if a proper explanation needs more than ~5 lines, point to a dedicated section/document instead of a vague name-drop. Naming `break` / `continue` / `null` / … in one sentence without a runnable example fails this bar. **Every runnable teaching example must show what it produces** next to the code (expected output, sample interactive session, or an explicit “compiles; no output” / “compile error” note) — code alone is not enough.
8. **Teaching examples** — Feature examples under `examples/` (and requirement worked samples when present) must be **elaborate enough to illustrate all or most aspects and variations** of the feature, not a single minimal happy path. Prefer several short, labeled sections over one opaque blob. Include **enough comments** that a reader can recover the teaching intent (what is being shown, why this form, expected print outcome where useful) without reading the ADR/CER. Runnable snippets in `examples/` and in the beginner book should show **expected output** (or compile-error / no-output) alongside the code, not only the source. When an example still misses a major variation after the feature lands, expand it in the same maturity push (or the immediate follow-up commit), matching the style of `examples/traits.pys` / `examples/switch.pys`. When the feature sits next to a sibling construct students confuse (`data` vs `struct`, `entity` vs `class`, …), the example must **spell out the use-case distinction** in comments and, where helpful, a short contrast snippet — not only the new keyword’s happy path.
9. **Railroad sync** — `docs/language-railroad.html` stays in sync with `docs/language.ebnf` whenever grammar/features change (same change set; the HTML is the navigable EBNF view, not an optional extra).
10. **Project memory** — CER and/or ADR written or amended in the same change set (see `project-memory.mdc`).
11. **No known maturity gaps left as “later”** unless the user **explicitly** defers them; if deferred, say so and list the gaps — never imply the feature is finished.
12. **Extension release (when cutting a tag)** — bump `pys-language/package.json` version and rewrite [`pys-language/RELEASE_NOTES.md`](../../pys-language/RELEASE_NOTES.md) for that version in the **same** change set on `main`; keep trunk green/releasable; tag only `extension-v<version>` from `main` tip. The publish workflow attaches the student zip + VSIX to a GitHub Release with those notes (Marketplace is optional when secrets exist). See [`pys-language/PUBLISH.md`](../../pys-language/PUBLISH.md).

## How to work

- Prefer delivering maturity in the same effort as the feature, not a vague follow-up.
- Incremental commits are fine; **claiming “implemented” is not** until the maturity checklist above is satisfied.
- When auditing an existing feature, report maturity honestly (what works / what’s missing) before adding more surface area.

## Anti-patterns

```text
❌ “Structs are implemented” after parse+emit only, with IDE/docs/field navigation still open
❌ EBNF updated but language-railroad.html still missing the new productions
❌ Language surface changed but book/ still shows old syntax or wrong mental model
❌ Lesson names break/continue (or similar) with no runnable example of each
❌ Teaching example shows only code with no expected output / error / “no output” note
❌ Using #i{…} / lambda / await in an early chapter before that concept is taught
❌ Closing a plan todo as done while listing “optional later” gaps that block teaching use
❌ Shipping examples/foo.pys that only demos one happy path while the feature has several forms
✅ “Implemented” only when language + tests + IDE parity + docs + beginner book (+ HTML) + railroad + CER/ADR + teaching examples (+ CI) are in place
✅ Book chapter done only when every named concept is explained + exemplified (incl. non-golden cases) with visible results
```
